{# Network -> Android TV activity mapping. TEMPLATE / EXAMPLE ONLY.

   Copy to streaming_apps.jinja (same directory) and fill in the services you
   actually subscribe to. The real file is gitignored, because which networks are
   mapped reveals a household's subscriptions.

   app_for(network) takes a TeamTracker `tv_network` attribute value (e.g. "NBC",
   "Prime Video", "FS1", "Apple TV") and returns one of:
     - an Android TV package name        e.g. com.example.streamingapp
     - a deep link URI                   e.g. examplesports://live
     - the string "unsupported"          -> automation notifies instead of launching

   Find real values on your own device rather than guessing:
     action: androidtv.adb_command
     data:
       command: "pm list packages | grep -iE 'peacock|espn|fox'"
     # then, for deep links:
       command: "cmd package dump <package> | grep -oE 'Scheme: \"[^\"]+\"' | sort -u"

   Read the result from the entity's `adb_response` attribute.

   Matching is substring-based on the lowercased network name, so one branch can
   cover several channels. Order matters: first match wins. Keep a catch-all
   `else` so an unrecognized network still lands somewhere sensible - a live-TV
   service carrying most linear channels is a good default.

   After editing the real file:
     action: homeassistant.reload_custom_templates
#}

{%- macro app_for(network) -%}
  {%- set n = (network | default('', true)) | lower -%}
  {%- if   'example-unavailable' in n %}unsupported
  {%- elif 'example-network' in n %}com.example.streamingapp
  {%- elif 'example-sports' in n %}examplesports://live
  {%- else %}com.example.livetv
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
